sdl3-mixer-fluidsynth
=====================

This crate is a modified version of FluidSynth: a translation into Rust of
parts of FluidSynth 2.5.7 (the SoundFont synthesizer, Copyright (C) 2003
Peter Hanappe and others; see its AUTHORS), as SDL_mixer 3.3.0 vendors it
(external/fluidsynth at SDL_mixer revision
df66ae893c91b0f6fa5d026a195bb278213d007d, https://github.com/libsdl-org/SDL_mixer,
which is https://github.com/libsdl-org/fluidsynth at revision
b53de2809bc5e4bffdaec59f447f81ba55ce4fe1), and of the parts of gcem 1.18.0
(the GCE-Math C++ library, Copyright (C) 2016-2024 Keith O'Hara) that
FluidSynth computes its lookup tables with, as FluidSynth vendors it there
(sdlorg_fork/gcem).

It is NOT the original FluidSynth or gcem, nor SDL_mixer's copies of them.

How and when they were changed: on 2026-10-17, the parts of FluidSynth's C
and C++ sources that SDL_mixer's FluidSynth decoder
(src/decoder_fluidsynth.c) reaches, built as SDL_mixer's CMake configures
FluidSynth (double-precision samples, no libsndfile, no threads, no audio
or MIDI drivers), were translated function by function into the Rust source
files in src/, which keep the original copyright notices and comments: the
synthesizer, its voices, channels, generators, modulators and tunings, the
rvoice DSP, mixer, reverb and chorus, the SoundFont 2 loader and sample
cache, the MIDI file player, the settings and the lookup tables (with the
gcem functions they are computed with, in src/gcem.rs). On 2026-11-23, the
native DLS loader (src/sfloader/fluid_dls.cpp, with the DLS default
modulators of src/synth/fluid_synth.c), which SDL_mixer's FluidSynth builds
in but SDL_mixer can't reach, was translated the same way, into
src/sfloader/dls.rs: it reads the DLS files a synth loads by their path.
Each file says so in a line under its copyright notice. The translation
works on Rust types (vectors, indices and reference counts rather than
pointers and linked lists, a mutex for the synth's recursive API lock), and
does something defined where corrupt input makes the C code read or write
out of bounds, convert out of range or loop forever (the places marked
"FIXME (upstream)" in the source). It also fixes bugs of the DLS loader
(marked "Note (upstream)"; upstream's C was patched the same way for the
tests): an articulation list whose condition is false is bypassed, where
upstream fails the whole file; a conditional expression's QUERY pushes its
value only, where upstream also pushes the "supported" flag over it; and an
instrument's Key Number generator is taken from its first articulation list
too, where upstream takes it from a second one only. Otherwise it renders
bit-for-bit what the C code does, which its tests check against upstream's
C built with the same math functions (upstream compiles its IIR filter with
-ffast-math, whose results depend on the compiler and the CPU; the
translation evaluates the filter as written). Left out, as SDL_mixer
doesn't reach them: the audio and MIDI drivers, the shell and server, the
sequencer, the MIDI router, LADSPA, compressed (SF3) samples (which a build
without libsndfile ignores too), the player's system timer, the settings'
realtime callbacks, SoundFont unloading and most of the remaining public
API. Of the DLS loader, left out are its libsndfile path (waves in other
formats than PCM, which a build without libsndfile fails on too, as the
translation does) and pinning its sample data to RAM (mlock(), whose
failure upstream only warns about; the SoundFont 2 sample cache leaves it
out too). Where the DLS loader's messages are the C++ library's (the what()
of std::bad_alloc, std::exception and vector::at()'s std::out_of_range),
the translation has libstdc++'s strings, the C++ library upstream's C was
checked with; no code of the C++ library is translated. The changes are
freely available as part of https://github.com/danielpclark/SDL.

License
-------

FluidSynth is licensed under the terms of the GNU Lesser General Public
License, version 2.1 or (at your option) any later version: see COPYING
(FluidSynth's LICENSE, verbatim from SDL_mixer). Its source files say so in
their headers; the few without a header (fluid_lfo.c, fluid_dls.cpp, the
gentables sources and some headers) are covered by that LICENSE as part of
FluidSynth. In fluid_dls.cpp, string_format() is based on a StackOverflow
answer under CC0 1.0 (the translation formats with Rust's format! instead),
and the DLSID values are those of the DLS Level 2 header file, dls2.h
("Written by Microsoft 1998. Released for public use."). gcem is licensed
under the Apache License, version 2.0: see LICENSE.gcem (gcem's LICENSE,
verbatim from SDL_mixer). So is this crate: its SPDX license expression is
"LGPL-2.1-or-later AND Apache-2.0" (the translation of gcem, src/gcem.rs,
is under the Apache License; everything else is under the LGPL). The Apache
License 2.0 is compatible only with version 3 of the LGPL (the Free
Software Foundation considers it incompatible with version 2.1), so the
combined crate is effectively distributed under the terms of the LGPL 3.0
or later, which FluidSynth's "or any later version" allows; the SPDX
expression stays the components'.

This is, with sdl3-mixer-timidity, one of the parts of the repository that
aren't under the zlib license. sdl3-mixer uses it to play MIDI through its
`fluidsynth` Cargo feature, which is on by default; build sdl3-mixer with
`default-features = false` to leave it (and MIDI) out.

No SoundFonts or DLS files are bundled. The test data in testdata/ (a
small synthetic SoundFont, synthetic DLS files written from the DLS
specifications, MIDI files, broken copies of them, and the list of test
cases) is generated by tools/gen_fluidsynth_testdata.py in the repository.
